course: dictionary
lesson: 5045
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word inn is a good example. «Hungry, he walks into the Red Lion inn in a little town and orders the best lunch they have.» Here an inn is a small hotel or pub where you can eat, drink and stay.
explain: Now read this: «There was no room for them at the inn.» This sentence comes from the Christmas story, where Mary and Joseph could not find a place to stay in Bethlehem. The clues are the story words, such as Bethlehem and the baby, so this inn is a place for travellers to stay the night.
explain: Ask yourself: what does the sentence tell me about the place? Is it for eating, sleeping, travellers, or cars? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. If «a small hotel or pub» still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Inn is a noun: an inn, inns.
fact: An inn is a small hotel or pub, often in the countryside or an old building, where travellers can eat, drink and stay the night.
fact: In the Christmas story, there was no room for Mary and Joseph at the inn in Bethlehem.
fact: An innkeeper is the person who runs an inn.
fact: Many British pubs have names like «The Red Lion Inn», where inn is part of the name.
exercise: «A motor-car pulls into the inn-yard and its owners come in, chattering.» What is the inn-yard? | *The yard of a small hotel or pub | A market for animals | A school playground | A field where sheep graze
exercise: «Caravans of camels rested at inns about a day's march apart.» What does inn mean here? | A type of camel | A wooden church where people pray | *A stopping place for travellers to eat and sleep | A dry, sandy desert
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to inn? | Castle | Trough | Desert | *Hotel
test: «There was no room at the inn, so the baby was laid in a manger.» What does inn mean in this sentence? | A room in a large house | *A stopping place for travellers, in the Christmas story | A kind of animal feed | A dark cave for animals
test: «Hungry, he walks into the Red Lion inn and orders lunch.» Why is the Red Lion called an inn? | Because a lion lives there | Because it is in a desert | *Because it is a small hotel or pub where you can eat and stay | Because it is a church with a bell
exam: Jayden says inn always means a place in the Christmas story. Is he right? | No, inns are only for camels | Yes, inns only appear in the Bible | *No, it can be a small hotel or pub, and the context shows which meaning | Yes, every inn was in Bethlehem
